    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-026-03500-4",
    "abstract": "Abstract How will climate change affect the composition of the seabed? Using three-dimensional hydrodynamic and wave models combined with climate projections from the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project phase 5, we investigate and quantify the drivers of seafloor stresses, using a North Western European Shelf case study. This study maps the potential for grain size mobilisation in present-day and future conditions, highlighting areas where transport capacity is likely to change. Sea level rise alters both absolute water depth and tidal dynamics, changing the magnitude and timing of seabed stresses while simultaneously reducing exposure to waves and currents. In contrast, increasing storm intensity in a warming climate is expected to cause more frequent episodic disturbance of an otherwise increasingly stable seabed. These predicted changes and their potential impacts on the benthic environment are discussed in the context of growing anthropogenic pressures and the associated challenges for marine spatial management.",
    "field": "The impact of climate change on seabed mobility",
    "problem": "Existing models of seabed stress and sediment mobility only represent historical or present-day conditions, failing to account for the future combined impacts of climate change-driven sea level rise and increased storm intensity, which are critical for future-proof marine spatial management.",
    "method": "We use three-dimensional hydrodynamic and wave models combined with climate projections to investigate and quantify future changes in seabed stresses and potential for sediment mobilisation on the North Western European Shelf.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify future changes in sea-bed stress and sediment mobility driven by climate change-induced storms and sea-level rise, using hydrodynamic models in a North Western European Shelf case study.",
